nss_ipv4/nss_ipv6: bound the conn_sync_many count by the receive buffer

The sync-many response carries an array of per-connection statistics and
a count of how many it holds. Both the count and the size it is checked
against come out of the same firmware-written buffer:

	if (nicsm->count * sizeof(struct nss_ipv4_conn_sync) >= nicsm->size) {

so the test compares one firmware value with another. It is wrong even
for a well-behaved firmware: ECM seeds size to PAGE_SIZE, which is larger
than the response buffer, so counts that pass still overread.

Worse, the test guards only the driver's own loop. The handler also sets
ncm->cb to the registered sync-many callback, and ECM's

	for (i = 0; i < nicsm->count; i++)

in ecm_nss_ipv4_connection_sync_many_callback() has no check at all,
feeding every record into a connection-hash lookup and a conntrack
timeout update from NAPI context. With count = 0xffff and 116-byte
records that is ~7.4 MB read past the response buffer.

ncm->len cannot serve as the bound. The firmware answers in the request's
own buffer and leaves the header intact, and ECM sends the request with
len = sizeof(struct nss_ipv4_conn_sync_many_msg), which is the 8-byte
header in front of the flexible array. A reply with records therefore
still reads len = 8, and a bound derived from it clamps every periodic
sync to zero records: only the single CONN_STATS_SYNC at teardown reaches
ECM, so per-connection totals, the bridge, VLAN and wireless interface
counters, conntrack accounting and the conntrack timeout refresh all
stand still while a connection is accelerated. On a GL-B3000 with the
12.2 firmware br-lan rx stayed flat through a 30 s upload and moved once,
by one sync period, at the end. On an AX3600 with the 12.5 firmware the
replies carry len 8 in receive buffers of 280 and 512 bytes, holding 2
and 4 116-byte records behind the 48-byte header.

Bound the count where both consumers route through it - the message
handler - by the receive buffer length nss_core_handle_nss_status_pkt()
validated, recorded in the context for the duration of the callback, and
drop the test it replaces. Clamping rather than returning is what keeps
ECM's unguarded loop safe without editing ECM. The clamp warns through
pr_warn_ratelimited() because nss_warning() is compiled out at the
default NSS_DEBUG_LEVEL.

Both families change together; the IPv4 and IPv6 paths are line-for-line
identical here.

